{"duration_api_ms":30498,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"af19f57b-dd62-4522-acc5-977df3feabb8","total_cost_usd":0.245064,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12881,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":4369,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1567},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12881,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4369,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12881},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6954,"outputTokens":13,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.007019,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":4369,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12881,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.238045,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":1567,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"preference","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"# The Carry Question: How a Pack Carries Weight Matters More Than What It Weighs\n\n## Scene 1\nVisual: Stock footage of a competitor rucking uphill at dawn with a loaded pack, shot from behind. Slow push-in on the shoulder straps.\nOn-screen text: As of October 15, 2026 | New pack reviews: how weight rides\nNarration: If you're headed to Mammoth, everything you use gets carried on foot. So the question isn't only how much a pack weighs. It's how that weight rides when you're tired. Three new pack reviews point at the same thing. Here's what they show, and what they don't.\nClaims: [01-c9]\n\n## Scene 2\nVisual: Close-up of a KUIU Icon 6 frame and pack, with a simple graphic of an older Icon frame beside a new one, marked \"not interchangeable.\"\nOn-screen text: Source: Rokslide review, Sept 23 | Rokslide: practitioner, ad-supported\nNarration: First, the KUIU Icon 6. Rokslide's reviewer reports it was designed from scratch. The new packs and frames are not backward compatible with older Icons. KUIU says the new frame brings more strength and durability. That's a company claim, relayed by the reviewer. He also notes that under really heavy loads, earlier Icon frames got overwhelmed, and some hunters saw frames crack and break. The excerpts we have don't show independent durability testing of the new frame.\nClaims: [01-c1] [01-c2]\n\n## Scene 3\nVisual: Footage of a hiker on a steep trail loop with a heavy pack. Overlay a simple graphic: \"1.2-mile loops, 510 feet up and down.\"\nOn-screen text: 28 lb day gear, then plus 64 lb simulated load | Empty weight: 5 lb 12.5 oz\nNarration: The same reviewer walked one point two mile loops, with five hundred ten feet of climbing and descending. He carried twenty-eight pounds of day gear, then added a sixty-four pound simulated elk quarter. He says the Icon 6 completely remedied the fatigue he'd had with earlier Icons under heavy loads. He also weighed it with the accessories he'd actually use: five pounds, twelve and a half ounces. He calls that in line with the Stone Glacier Sky fifty-nine hundred. So on weight, it's no standout. The difference he describes is in how it carries.\nClaims: [01-c3] [01-c4]\n\n## Scene 4\nVisual: Close-up of a GORUCK Rucker 5.0 with a weight plate sliding into the internal plate pocket. Trail footage in the woods.\nOn-screen text: Source: Ruck Authority, Rucker 5.0 review, at 3:00 | Bag provided free; affiliate links\nNarration: Next, the GORUCK Rucker five point oh. Ruck Authority's reviewer says GORUCK sent him the bag, though they didn't tell him what to say. The review also uses affiliate links, so weigh his praise with that in mind. He tested it for a week on Wisconsin trails with a twenty pound plate, and without the optional hip belt. He called it rock solid, with no wobbling. He reported no hot spots, chafing, numbness, or lower back discomfort. His traps were a little sore, and he thought a hip belt might help.\nClaims: [01-c5] [01-c6]\n\n## Scene 5\nVisual: Side-by-side comparison graphic of the GORUCK GR1 and the Rucker 5.0, with labels \"sternum strap\" and \"plate pockets.\"\nOn-screen text: Source: Ruck Authority, GR1 review, at 2:05 and 3:06\nNarration: The same reviewer also tested the GORUCK G R One. At twenty pounds, he says it worked without a chest strap. But he adds that plenty of experienced ruckers will tell you a sternum strap matters as weight goes up, and he isn't arguing with them. His conclusion: for pure rucking, the Rucker five point oh takes the win.\nClaims: [01-c7]\n\n## Scene 6\nVisual: Trail runner descending a hillside wearing a small running vest. Graphic callout on the lack of a hip belt.\nOn-screen text: Source: Treeline Review, Distance 15 | Independent, affiliate model\nNarration: Third, the Black Diamond Distance fifteen, a light running vest. Treeline Review's reviewer reports that when heavily loaded, it bounces as you run, especially downhill. It has no hip belt. It's a fifteen liter vest, and the reviewer says it isn't meant for overnight loads of twenty liters or more. Treeline uses affiliate links.\nClaims: [01-c8]\n\n## Scene 7\nVisual: Simple three-column graphic: Icon 6, Rucker 5.0, Distance 15. Under each, the word from the review: \"frame,\" \"stable,\" \"bounces.\"\nOn-screen text: Our interpretation: fit, suspension, and load stability\nNarration: Here's our take. These are three different packs from three separate publishers, so this is a pattern, not proof about any one product. The reviewers' common thread isn't weight. It's stability and comfort under load. Put together, this suggests fit, suspension, and load stability are the variables worth your attention. That's our reading. None of these reviewers says it.\nClaims: [01-c9]\n\n## Scene 8\nVisual: Plain graphic with a short checklist that appears line by line.\nOn-screen text: What this evidence can't tell us\nNarration: Now, the limits. None of these tests happened at Mammoth, or over multi-day team carries. The Icon 6 loops were one point two miles. The Ruck Authority tests used a twenty pound plate over roughly one to two weeks of casual trail use. The Distance 15 is a running vest, and its own reviewer says it isn't for overnight loads. KUIU's claims reach us only through Rokslide. We have no Mammoth organizer, past competitor, or lab data. The official Mammoth page wasn't supplied, so we're not stating what's required. And these are excerpts, so omitted passages may add qualifications.\nClaims: [01-c2] [01-c5] [01-c8] [01-c9]\n\n## Scene 9\nVisual: Competitor shouldering a loaded pack at a trailhead, then end card with the publication name.\nOn-screen text: Full article and sources linked in the description | Produced with AI assistance from cited sources\nNarration: So here's the takeaway. When you look at new packs, ask how the load rides, not only what the pack weighs. Then test that with your own gear, at the weight you plan to carry, for far longer than these reviewers did. The full article and every source are linked in the description. This video was produced with AI assistance from cited sources. Thanks for watching.\nClaims: [01-c9]\n\n## YouTube Description\nThis video looks at three newly reviewed packs: the KUIU Icon 6, the GORUCK Rucker 5.0 and G R One, and the Black Diamond Distance 15. Across separate reviews, the common thread is how weight rides under load, not empty weight. Every test used loads far below a multi-day Mammoth carry, so the reviews show direction, not proof of Mammoth suitability.\n\nFull article: [ARTICLE LINK]\n\nSources:\n- Rokslide, \"KUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review,\" September 23, 2026. https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/\n- Ruck Authority, \"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: My First Week on the Trail,\" September 24, 2026. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVz1KOKvi-I (cited at 0:04, 1:03, 1:33, and 3:00)\n- Ruck Authority, \"GORUCK GR1 26L Review: An Everyday Bag That Can Ruck,\" October 2, 2026. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SlW6o3cO2rk (cited at 2:05 and 3:06)\n- Treeline Review, \"I tested the Black Diamond Distance 15 Backpack while running mountains across Colorado,\" October 1, 2026. https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/black-diamond-distance-15-backpack-review\n\nThis video was produced with AI assistance from cited sources and reviewed by a human editor.","ttft_ms":12427,"type":"result","duration_ms":29775,"uuid":"449cc4f7-6d97-4415-bad4-3649969f9620","ttft_stream_ms":1357,"time_to_request_ms":65,"first_content_frame_ms":1357,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
